output:
In this French study, pain typically started around age 10, became long-lasting around age 20, but hEDS wasn't diagnosed until around age 24. Almost everyone (97%) had severe chronic pain, and in 75% it gradually increased over time. It was mostly felt in the joints, especially in the legs.

It's complicated because there are usually several kinds of pain at once:
- pain from joints and tissues;
- nerve pain;
- often a mix of the two, sometimes with the whole body becoming more sensitive to pain.

Because everyone's mix is different, pain treatment should be tailored to you, so it helps to describe your pain in detail to your doctor.

POTS is the most prevalent autonomic profile in hEDS. Neurally mediated hypotension and orthostatic intolerance are also common.

Cardiovascular dysautonomia can explain:
- orthostatic intolerance, palpitations and tachycardia;
- atypical chest pain;
- fatigue, dizziness, presyncope and syncope;
- memory and concentration problems.

Reduced sweat production (sudomotor involvement) has been demonstrated and may explain dry skin and mucosa. Gut dysmotility and bladder dysfunction may share an autonomic component.

The proposed main mechanism is sympathetic neurogenic dysfunction. Connective tissue laxity of vessels and vasoactive medications may contribute.
